From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:07:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia: keep uncached and write-combined sysmem pages
 uncached in a pool

Every uncached or write-combined system memory allocation changes the
page attributes of the kernel mapping to UC- (set_pages_array_uc) and
back to write-back on free, each a TLB shootdown IPI to every CPU and a
cache flush. Measured ~200 us per allocation regardless of size, so a
GL/Vulkan client start spent 4-7 ms in these conversions (72-144 of
them) while holding the RM API lock in write mode, blocking every other
RM client including the compositor. The sysmem page pool did not help:
it only holds write-back pages, so pooled pages were converted again.

Add a second set of per node and order pools, flagged uncached, whose
pages stay UC-:
- free: an uncached allocation whose every OS page is uncached (no
  partial last page; contiguous only for single pages) goes into the
  uncached pool as is, all or nothing, if the pool stays below
  uncached_pool_max_mb. Otherwise it is converted and freed as before.
- scrub: the pool worker clears dirty pages through a temporary
  write-combined vmap (the kernel mapping is uncached, clearing through
  it would be slow), and allocation only takes clean pages from it.
- alloc: pages from the uncached pool fill the allocation first, the
  rest comes from the write-back pool or the page allocator and only
  that part is converted. If the allocation fails, the pool pages are
  converted back and everything is freed as cached (which also stops
  converting never-converted pages back on that path).
- reclaim and teardown: the shrinker and pool destroy convert pages back
  to write-back, in batches of 32, before freeing them.

Only built where set_pages_array_uc() is used, and only with a single
online NUMA node, like the existing pooling of uncached allocations.

nvidia.uncached_pool_max_mb sets the cap (default 256, runtime writable;
0 disables the pool, pooled pages are then released by the shrinker).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 kernel-open/nvidia/nv-vm.c | 338 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 302 insertions(+), 36 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-vm.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-vm.c
